    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d283">

                <pb facs="0818" n="802" sameAs="frus:frus1950v01/pg802"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">394.31/9–2650</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by Mr. Lucius D. Battle of
                        the Executive Secretariat of the Department of State</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1950-09-26">September 26, 1950</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <table>
                    <row role="label">
                        <cell rendition="#nowrap">Participants:</cell>
                        <cell>Mr. Acheson</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Bevin<anchor n="1" xml:id="d283-f1"/>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Barclay<anchor n="2" xml:id="d283-f2"/>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Battle</cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>In a conversation on other matters, Mr. Bevin mentioned the Torquay tariff
                    negotiations. He said that he hoped that the negotiations <pb facs="0819"
                        n="803" xml:id="pg803"/> this time would not get in a wrangle on the
                    question of imperial preferences. He said that the issue would not arise. He
                    said the British would do all possible to meet us on other matters but hoped
                    that the question of imperial preferences could be avoided.</p>
                <p>Mr. Acheson said that he would take the matter up with Willard Thorp.<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d283-f3"/>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d283-f1" n="1" xml:id="d283-f1-content">
                <p>Ernest Bevin, British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d283-f2" n="2" xml:id="d283-f2-content">
                <p>R. E. Barclay, Private Secretary to Mr. Bevin.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d283-f3" n="3" xml:id="d283-f3-content">
                <p>Willard L. Thorp, Assistant Secretary of State for Economic Affairs, had been
                    designated Chairman of the United States Delegation to the Third Bound of Tariff
                    Negotiations under the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade. For a list of
                    persons named to the United States Delegation, see Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, October 2, 1950, pp. 553 ff. The Third
                    Round opened at Torquay, England, on September 28.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
